##ALGORITHM: MATRIX CHAIN MULTIPLICATION

1. Start.

2. Read N.

3. Read the array arr[] containing the dimensions
   of the matrices.

4. Create a DP table dp[N][N].

5. Set dp[i][i] = 0 for all i because a single
   matrix requires no multiplication.

6. Consider chain lengths from 2 to N-1.

7. For every starting position i:

      Calculate j = i + length - 1.

8. Set dp[i][j] = infinity.

9. Try every possible splitting position k
   from i to j-1.

10. Calculate:

      cost = dp[i][k] + dp[k+1][j]
             + arr[i-1] * arr[k] * arr[j]

11. If cost is smaller than dp[i][j],
    update dp[i][j].

12. After filling the table, dp[1][N-1]
    contains the minimum number of scalar
    multiplications.

13. Display the result.

14. Stop.



**Time Complexity Analysis:**

There are three nested loops in the algorithm.
The chain length loop takes O(N) time,
the starting position loop takes O(N) time,
and the splitting position loop takes O(N) time.

Therefore,

Time Complexity = O(N) × O(N) × O(N)
                = O(N^3)


Space Complexity Analysis:

A two-dimensional DP table of size N × N is used.

Therefore,

Space Complexity = O(N × N)
                 = O(N^2)
